# CodeRabbit + merge loops (`dev` → `main`)

Use this with **pull-requests.mdc** whenever the user runs `/loop`, autopilot, or “until merge” on the open **`dev` → `main`** PR.

**Autopilot vs merge:** If the user granted merge in `/loop` (or equivalent), **ignore** generic autopilot “never merge” guidance — follow **Merge authority** below.

## Resolve threads yourself (prevent infinite loops)

CodeRabbit’s GitHub integration is **unreliable**: it often **does not** resolve review threads after you push a fix, and the next review can re-flag the same item while the thread stays open. That produces **infinite `/loop` cycles** (fix → push → wait → triage same comment again).

**Whenever you implement, dismiss, or verify a CodeRabbit suggestion** (inline thread, outside-diff block, or summary item):

1. **Resolve the matching GitHub review thread after the fix is tested and pushed** — in the same turn as the fix, before you arm the watcher or end the turn. If no commit is needed, verify the existing code first. Do **not** wait for CodeRabbit to close it.
2. If there is no thread (outside-diff-only), treat the item as done once code on HEAD satisfies it; do not re-implement on the next wake unless HEAD regressed.
3. Optionally reply on the thread: `Fixed in <short-sha>` or `Already satisfied on HEAD`.

**Order on each implemented fix:** triage → fix → test → commit/push → **resolve thread** → verify thread state → watcher. For a dismissal or an already-satisfied finding, verify it before resolving the thread. Never push a CodeRabbit fix and leave its thread open “for CodeRabbit.”

## Turn exit contract (mandatory for `/loop` and merge intent)

Do **not** end a turn leaving the PR loop “half done”. Before you stop:

1. **If you just pushed to `dev`** and CI or CodeRabbit can still be running → you **must wait or arm the watcher** (below). Never use a **capped** poll (`for i in 1..10; sleep 30`) and then exit.
2. **If merge-ready** (checklist below on current `headRefOid`) and the user authorized merge → **`gh pr merge N --merge` in this turn**, then stop watchers.
3. **If CodeRabbit finished** with actionable items → **triage and fix (or dismiss) in this turn**, push if needed, then wait or merge — do not report “will continue later” without a live watcher.
4. **If nothing is left to do except waiting on GitHub** → run **`./scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh <N> --wait`** in the foreground with **`block_until_ms` ≥ 900000**, **or** start the same command in the background with **`notify_on_output`** on `^AGENT_LOOP_WAKE_pr-merge`. A blind `sleep 90` heartbeat alone is **not** sufficient after a push.

On every wake (including timer/heartbeat), read **`scripts/.pr-loop-state.json`** if present, re-run checklist on the **current** PR head, then continue A–G.

## Local watcher (`scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh`)

Repo-owned wait for **`rust`**, **`native-and-ts`**, and **CodeRabbit** on the open PR.

```bash
# Quick status (non-blocking)
./scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh <N>

# Block until CI + CodeRabbit idle (preferred after push)
./scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh <N> --wait
```

- **Foreground:** Shell `block_until_ms` **≥ 900000** (15m); when the script prints `AGENT_LOOP_WAKE_pr-merge`, run triage A–G immediately in the **same** or **next** turn (same session).
- **Background:** `block_until_ms: 0`, `notify_on_output` pattern `^AGENT_LOOP_WAKE_pr-merge`, **one** watcher per PR; kill **`scripts/.pr-loop-watch.pid`** when merging or when the user stops the loop.
- **Do not** stack multiple `while true; sleep …` heartbeats; use this script only.

When the wake reason is **`ready_to_triage`**: parse CodeRabbit review bodies (including **Outside diff range comments**), fix, push only if needed, resolve threads, then merge if checklist passes.

## Merge authority

**Merge without asking** when the user:

- Says merge if ready / don’t wait for confirmation / loop until merge / autopilot until merged, or
- Includes merge intent in the `/loop` prompt.

**Do not merge** only when the user **explicitly** forbids it in the same thread (e.g. “do not merge”, “review only”).

When merge-ready (below), run `gh pr merge <number> --merge` in the **same pass**. Do not report “ready for merge” and arm another long sleep if nothing is left to fix.

## Merge-ready checklist (all required)

1. PR is **OPEN**, **MERGEABLE**, merge state **CLEAN** (or equivalent).
2. Required CI **success**: at minimum **`rust`** and **`native-and-ts`** on the current PR head.
3. **CodeRabbit** check is **`pass`** / **`success`** on the current head (treat `pending`, `in progress`, `queued`, `fail`, `failure`, and any other state as **not** complete).
4. **No actionable CodeRabbit findings** left after full triage (inline **and** outside-diff — see below).
5. **All** GitHub **review threads resolved** (you may resolve them after verifying the fix).

If (1)–(4) hold but (5) has stale open threads for code already fixed: **resolve those threads**, then merge.

If you push fixes: re-run the checklist on the **new** head (wait for CI + CodeRabbit idle before merge).

## CodeRabbit triage — every loop wake (mandatory)

**Inline-only triage is incomplete.** CodeRabbit often puts findings only in the **review summary** under **Outside diff range comments** (GitHub cannot attach them to the diff). Those never show up as unresolved inline threads.

### A. Refresh PR head and checks

```bash
gh pr view <n> --json number,headRefOid,mergeable,mergeStateStatus,state,statusCheckRollup
gh pr checks <n>
```

### B. Unresolved review threads (GraphQL)

Paginate **every** GitHub collection before merge: GraphQL `reviewThreads` with cursor pagination (`first` + `after` until `hasNextPage` is false), and REST with `gh api --paginate` for pull comments and review lists.

```graphql
query($cursor: String) { repository(owner:"OWNER", name:"REPO") { pullRequest(number:N) {
  reviewThreads(first:100, after: $cursor) { pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    nodes { id isResolved path line
    comments(last:1) { nodes { body author { login } } } } } } } }
```

Fix or dismiss each unresolved thread; then resolve via:

```graphql
mutation { resolveReviewThread(input: {threadId: "PRRT_..."}) { thread { isResolved } } }
```

### C. Pull request inline comments

```bash
gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/N/comments
```

Include comments on **older commits** if the finding still applies to current code. Ignore bodies that say **Addressed in commit …** only after you **verify** on HEAD.

### D. CodeRabbit review bodies (outside diff + summary)

```bash
gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/N/reviews \
  --jq '.[] | select(.user.login|test("coderabbit";"i")) | {id, commit_id, submitted_at, body}'
```

For **each** CodeRabbit review (prioritize reviews whose `commit_id` matches PR head, then the newest):

1. If the body contains **`Outside diff range comments`** or **outside the diff**, read **every** nested block (usually `<details><summary>… · <code>path:line</code>`). Extract path and line range; implement valid fixes on `dev`.
2. Note **`Actionable comments posted: N`**. If N > 0, ensure all N are accounted for (inline + outside-diff), not only open threads.
3. Treat **Minor**, **Quick win**, and **Major** the same for implementation: if the finding is still valid, fix it. Do not skip “minor” items to save time.

Example review URL pattern: `pullrequestreview-<id>` → fetch with:

```bash
gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/N/reviews/<review_id> --jq .body
```

### E. Fix, resolve, test, changelog

For **each** CodeRabbit item you address in this wake:

- Smallest correct diff on **`dev`**; run scoped tests.
- Update root **CHANGELOG.md** `[Unreleased]` for user-facing package/crate changes.
- Commit, push **`dev`**; **`gh pr edit`** if PR scope/title/body needs refresh.
- **Resolve** the linked review thread (mutation in **B**) after the fix is on the remote PR head (or after verify-only dismiss).
- Re-check **B**: any thread you fixed this turn must show `isResolved: true` before you start `./scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh`.

If code already matches the suggestion (no commit needed), **resolve the thread anyway** and do not re-edit.

### F. Resolve threads proactively

Same rule as **Resolve threads yourself** above: CodeRabbit will **not** reliably close threads for you. Leaving them open causes the merge loop to treat the PR as still actionable forever.

- Resolve via GraphQL after every implemented or dismissed finding.
- Do not leave threads open “for CodeRabbit” or “until the next review.”

### G. Merge or re-wait (same pass)

| State | Action |
|--------|--------|
| User granted merge + checklist pass on current head | **`gh pr merge N --merge`**; stop loop timers |
| Fixes just pushed | `./scripts/watch-pr-merge-loop.sh N --wait` (or background + notify); repeat A–G on new head |
| CodeRabbit still running | **Watcher only** — **do not** treat as merge-ready; **do not** end turn without `--wait` or background watcher |
| CI green + CodeRabbit idle + no actionable items + threads resolved | **Merge** (if merge authority); do not wait for user |

## Anti-patterns

- Ending a `/loop` turn after push while CodeRabbit/CI may still run, with only a vague “heartbeat” and no `watch-pr-merge-loop.sh`.
- Capped poll loops then exiting (e.g. ten × 30s) while CodeRabbit is still **Review in progress**.
- Polling for an hour after CI and CodeRabbit are already green with zero open findings.
- Triage only GraphQL unresolved threads and inline comments, **missing outside-diff** review text.
- Assuming “Review completed” means nothing left to do without parsing the latest review body.
- Skipping **Minor** / **Quick win** comments.
- Waiting for the user to say “merge” when the loop already authorized it.
- Pushing a CodeRabbit fix without resolving its thread in the same turn (common cause of infinite loop wakes).
- Re-implementing the same CodeRabbit comment on every wake because the thread was never resolved.
